2026-06-30 15:46:30,392 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-8d7f3227-0e7d-4726-9aa2-d071c3b48a8f', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the mass spectrometry acquisition.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: instrument\nsdrf_column: comment[instrument]\nontology: MS:1000031 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Mass spectrometer instrument make and model.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology. If multiple instruments, return a list.\n  Common instruments (name → accession):\n    Orbitrap Astral → MS:1003356\n    Orbitrap Exploris 480 → MS:1003028\n    Orbitrap Eclipse → MS:1003349\n    Orbitrap Fusion Lumos → MS:1002732\n    Orbitrap Fusion → MS:1002416\n    Q Exactive HF-X → MS:1002877\n    Q Exactive HF → MS:1002523\n    Q Exactive Plus → MS:1002634\n    Q Exactive → MS:1001911\n    LTQ Orbitrap Velos → MS:1001742\n    LTQ Orbitrap XL → MS:1000556\n    timsTOF → MS:1003005\n    timsTOF Pro → MS:1003230\n    TripleTOF 6600 → MS:1002583\n  Format: NT=<instrument name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: proteomics data acquisition method\nsdrf_column: comment[proteomics data acquisition method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000659\ndescription: Mass spectrometry data acquisition method.\ninstructions: For XL-MS this is nearly always DDA. Use:\n  NT=data-dependent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000041 — for DDA/shotgun\n  NT=data-independent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000449 — for DIA (rare in XL-MS)\n  NT=targeted acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000450 — for SRM/PRM\n  Default to DDA if not stated.\n\nterm: dissociation method\nsdrf_column: comment[dissociation method]\nontology: MS:1000044 (PSI-MS children)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nProximity labeling of trans-cinnamic acid 4-hydroxylase in Petunia inflata petal protoplasts\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nProximity-dependent labeling allows untargeted determination of both direct and indirect protein interactions in vivo, and therefore stands as an attractive alternative to targeted binary assays for determining global proteinprotein interaction networks. We used TurboID-based proximity labeling to study protein interaction networks of the core phenylpropanoid and anthocyanin pathways in petunia. To do so, we coupled the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) membrane anchored cytochrome P450 cinnamic acid 4-hydroxylase (C4H, CYP73A412) from Petunia inflata to TurboID and expressed it in protoplasts derived from anthocyanin-rich petunia petals. We identified multiple soluble enzymes from the late anthocyanin pathway among enriched proteins, along with other C4H isoforms, and other ER membrane anchored CYPs. Several of these interactions were subsequently confirmed by bimolecular fluorescence complementation (BiFC).\n\n[METHODS]\nThree biological replicates of each transformation with p35S::C4H-TurboID, p35S::ER-TurboID-EGFP (subcellular compartment control) and with buffer (WT control,unstransformed) were incubated in 2 mL TEX buffer containing 50 μM biotinfor 0 and 3 h at 25°C. Protoplasts were washed 5 times with ice-cold W5 solution (154 mM NaCl, 125 mM CaCl2, 5 mM KCl, 5 mM glucose, 2 mM MES, pH adjusted to 5.7), 100 x g centrifugation at 4°C between washes and pelleted after the last wash. To obtain total protein extracts, the protoplast pellet was lysed with ice-cold 200 μL RIPA buffer supplemented with proteinase inhibitor, 1 mM DTT and 1 mM phenylmethylsulfonyl fluoride (PMSF). The solution was incubatedon ice for 15 min and then centrifuged for 10 min, 4°C at 15000 x g. The supernatant was transferred to a protein Lo-Bind microcentrifuge tube (Eppendorf) and total protein was subjected to colorimetric quantification with Pierce™ BCA protein assay kit (Thermo Scientific). To capture biotinylated proteins, 100 μL slurry of magnetic beads conjugated with streptavidin (Thermo Fisher) was equilibrated by washing with RIPA buffer 3 times and subsequentlyincubated overnight with 100 μg of total protein extracted from petunia protoplasts, under continuous rotation at 4°C. After incubation, the beads were washed as follows: 3x with cold RIPA buffer supplemented with 1 mM DTT for 2 min, 1x with cold 1 M KCl, 1x with cold 0.1 M Na2CO3, 1x with 2 M urea in 10 mM Tris-HCl pH 8, 2x with cold RIPA buffer supplemented with 1 mM DTT and 3x with cold PBS to remove traces of detergents. One tenth of the beads was boiled in Laemmli buffer containing 20 mM DTT and 2 mM biotin and used for immunoblots. The rest of the beads were kept dried at -80°C until further processing. Washed beads were incubated for 30 min with elution buffer 1 (2 M Urea, 50 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.5, 2 mM DTT, 20 μg/ml trypsin) followed by a second elution for 5 min with elution buffer 2 (2 M Urea, 50 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.5, 10 mM Chloroacetamide). Both eluates were combined and further incubated at room temperature over-night. Tryptic peptide mixtures were acidified to 1% TFA and loaded on Evotips (Evosep, Odense, Denmark). Peptides were separated on 15 cm, 150 µm ID columns packed with C18 beads (1.9 mm) (Pepsep) on an Evosep ONE HPLC applying the ‘30 samples per day’ method and injected via a CaptiveSpray source with a 10 mm emitter into a timsTOF pro mass spectrometer (Bruker) operated in PASEF mode. Briefly, the DDA-PASEF scan range for both MS and MS/MS was set to 100 - 1700 m/z, and TIMS mobility range to 0.6 – 1.6 (Vcm−2). TIMS ramp and accumulation times were set to 100 ms each, and 10 PASEF ramps recorded for a total cycle time of 1.17sec. MS/MS target intensity and intensity threshold were set to 20.000 and 1.000, respectively. An exclusion list of 0.4 min for precursors within 0.015 m/z and 0.015 V cm−2 width was also activated.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
