2026-06-30 15:46:34,619 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-1878afa5-ca73-4b6b-a99b-a278d7129011', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\nFor each term, return the best available ontology-resolved value with its accession where indicated.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: organism\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism]\nontology: NCBITaxon (e.g. NCBITaxon:9606)\ndescription: Source species of the proteome of interest in the biological samples analyzed by mass spectrometry. Return the scientific name. \nSource species maybe different than host species when proteins from one organism are expressed in another. \ninstructions: Use NCBITaxon accession. Common examples: Homo sapiens (NCBITaxon:9606),\n  Mus musculus (NCBITaxon:10090), Saccharomyces cerevisiae (NCBITaxon:4932),\n  Escherichia coli (NCBITaxon:562). If multiple species, return a list.\n\nterm: organism part\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism part]\nontology: UBERON or BTO\ndescription: Main normalized anatomical term for the sample analyzed by mass spectrometry. \ninstructions: Use the clearest ontology-backed anatomy term. Examples: whole-cell lysate, mitochondria,\n  nucleus, cytoplasm, liver, heart, brain, cell, ect. Return null if not applicable\n  (e.g. purified recombinant proteins with no tissue origin).\n\nterm: cell line\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell line]\nontology: CLO, BTO, or EFO (e.g. CLO:0001230 for HEK293)\ndescription: Name of the cell line if a cell line was used.\ninstructions: Return null if no cell line was used (primary cells or tissues).\n\nterm: cell type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell type]\nontology: CL, BTO, or CLO\ndescription: Cell type of the sample.\ninstructions: Return null if not applicable or not stated.\n\nterm: material type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[material type]\nontology: PRIDE:0000837\ndescription: Type of biological material analyzed. Differentiates whether the whole cell lysate was analyzed, just a specific anatomical structure or tissue, or if the samples were purified for specific proteins before mass spectrometry analysis. \nallowed_values: tissue, whole cell lysate, organell, membrane, synthetic, purified proteins, affinity captured\ninstructions: Choose the best matching fixed value. Return null if unclear.\n\nterm: strain or breed\nsdrf_column: characteristics[strain or breed]\nontology: free text\ndescription: Strain, breed, cultivar, or ecotype of the organism.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: developmental stage\nsdrf_column: characteristics[developmental stage]\nontology: EFO\ndescription: Developmental stage of the organism or donor.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: technology type\nsdrf_column: technology type\nontology: PRIDE:0000663\ndescription: Type of technology used. For XL-MS this is always fixed.\nallowed_values: proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry" for XL-MS datasets.\n\nterm: source name\nsdrf_column: source name\nontology: PRIDE:0000623\ndescription: Unique identifier for the biological sample. Typically derived from\n  the dataset file names or sample descriptions in the paper.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention used (e.g. "PXD_stem" or "condition_replicate").\n  This is auto-generated from file names; return a description of the sample naming pattern only.\n\nterm: assay name\nsdrf_column: assay name\nontology: NCIT:C178857\ndescription: Unique identifier for each MS run/assay. Typically the raw file stem.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention. This is auto-generated; return the pattern only.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable (e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"), else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text supporting the extraction, or null\n  "evidence_location": where in the paper (e.g. "Methods", "Abstract"), or null\n\nExample output structure:\n{\n  "characteristics[organism]": {"value": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae", "accession": "NCBITaxon:4932", "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  "characteristics[organism part]": {"value": "mitochondria", "accession": null, "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  ...\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nProximity labeling of trans-cinnamic acid 4-hydroxylase in Petunia inflata petal protoplasts\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nProximity-dependent labeling allows untargeted determination of both direct and indirect protein interactions in vivo, and therefore stands as an attractive alternative to targeted binary assays for determining global proteinprotein interaction networks. We used TurboID-based proximity labeling to study protein interaction networks of the core phenylpropanoid and anthocyanin pathways in petunia. To do so, we coupled the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) membrane anchored cytochrome P450 cinnamic acid 4-hydroxylase (C4H, CYP73A412) from Petunia inflata to TurboID and expressed it in protoplasts derived from anthocyanin-rich petunia petals. We identified multiple soluble enzymes from the late anthocyanin pathway among enriched proteins, along with other C4H isoforms, and other ER membrane anchored CYPs. Several of these interactions were subsequently confirmed by bimolecular fluorescence complementation (BiFC).\n\n[METHODS]\nThree biological replicates of each transformation with p35S::C4H-TurboID, p35S::ER-TurboID-EGFP (subcellular compartment control) and with buffer (WT control,unstransformed) were incubated in 2 mL TEX buffer containing 50 μM biotinfor 0 and 3 h at 25°C. Protoplasts were washed 5 times with ice-cold W5 solution (154 mM NaCl, 125 mM CaCl2, 5 mM KCl, 5 mM glucose, 2 mM MES, pH adjusted to 5.7), 100 x g centrifugation at 4°C between washes and pelleted after the last wash. To obtain total protein extracts, the protoplast pellet was lysed with ice-cold 200 μL RIPA buffer supplemented with proteinase inhibitor, 1 mM DTT and 1 mM phenylmethylsulfonyl fluoride (PMSF). The solution was incubatedon ice for 15 min and then centrifuged for 10 min, 4°C at 15000 x g. The supernatant was transferred to a protein Lo-Bind microcentrifuge tube (Eppendorf) and total protein was subjected to colorimetric quantification with Pierce™ BCA protein assay kit (Thermo Scientific). To capture biotinylated proteins, 100 μL slurry of magnetic beads conjugated with streptavidin (Thermo Fisher) was equilibrated by washing with RIPA buffer 3 times and subsequentlyincubated overnight with 100 μg of total protein extracted from petunia protoplasts, under continuous rotation at 4°C. After incubation, the beads were washed as follows: 3x with cold RIPA buffer supplemented with 1 mM DTT for 2 min, 1x with cold 1 M KCl, 1x with cold 0.1 M Na2CO3, 1x with 2 M urea in 10 mM Tris-HCl pH 8, 2x with cold RIPA buffer supplemented with 1 mM DTT and 3x with cold PBS to remove traces of detergents. One tenth of the beads was boiled in Laemmli buffer containing 20 mM DTT and 2 mM biotin and used for immunoblots. The rest of the beads were kept dried at -80°C until further processing. Washed beads were incubated for 30 min with elution buffer 1 (2 M Urea, 50 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.5, 2 mM DTT, 20 μg/ml trypsin) followed by a second elution for 5 min with elution buffer 2 (2 M Urea, 50 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.5, 10 mM Chloroacetamide). Both eluates were combined and further incubated at room temperature over-night. Tryptic peptide mixtures were acidified to 1% TFA and loaded on Evotips (Evosep, Odense, Denmark). Peptides were separated on 15 cm, 150 µm ID columns packed with C18 beads (1.9 mm) (Pepsep) on an Evosep ONE HPLC applying the ‘30 samples per day’ method and injected via a CaptiveSpray source with a 10 mm emitter into a timsTOF pro mass spectrometer (Bruker) operated in PASEF mode. Briefly, the DDA-PASEF scan range for both MS and MS/MS was set to 100 - 1700 m/z, and TIMS mobility range to 0.6 – 1.6 (Vcm−2). TIMS ramp and accumulation times were set to 100 ms each, and 10 PASEF ramps recorded for a total cycle time of 1.17sec. MS/MS target intensity and intensity threshold were set to 20.000 and 1.000, respectively. An exclusion list of 0.4 min for precursors within 0.015 m/z and 0.015 V cm−2 width was also activated.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
